   ],
   "l": "Heaven lasting, earth enduring.\nThat whereby heaven-and-earth can be lasting and enduring besides —\nbecause they do not live for themselves,",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Heaven lasts and earth endures. What lets heaven and earth last and endure is that they do not live for themselves —",
   "n": "天長地久 pulls apart the very compound the next line puts back together: 天地 is a fixed pair, 'heaven-and-earth', but here 天 and 地 are split into a matched couplet, each with its own predicate. 所以…者 is a frame around a whole clause — 所以 'that by which', 者 closing it and turning the clause into a noun: 'the means by which heaven and earth can be long and enduring'. Then 以 opens the answer, and note that this 以 is 'because of', not the 'thereby, so as to' it was in chapter 1; the same character does both jobs. 自生 can be heard two ways, 'live for themselves' or 'give life to themselves'. Wang Bi takes the first: living for themselves, they would be in contention with things; not living for themselves, things come home to them.",
   "p": true
  },
