   "l": "therefore they can live long.\nFor this reason the sage puts his person behind, and the person comes first;\nputs his person outside, and the person is there still.\nIs it not because of his being without private ends?\nTherefore he can complete his own private ends.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "and so they live long. This is why the sage sets himself behind, and thereby stands in front; leaves himself out of account, and thereby survives. Is it not because he has no ends of his own that his own ends are fulfilled?",
   "n": "後 and 外 are place-words — 'behind', 'outside' — put to work as transitive verbs: 'to put behind', 'to set outside'. Nothing marks the change; only position does. This is the part-of-speech fluidity you met in chapter 1's 道可道, in a new guise, and it is everywhere. 邪 is the trap in this chapter: here it is not xié 'evil' but the final particle yé, equivalent to 耶, which turns the line into a rhetorical question — 'is it not because…?' Some editions print 耶 outright. The close turns on one character used twice: he has no 私, no ends of his own, and just so his 私, what is his own, is brought to completion. 先 and 存 fall in the same Old Chinese rhyme group, so the couplet closed on a rhyme — 身先／身存 — that Mandarin (xiān / cún) has lost. Beyond that the chapter binds itself by repeating its end-words: 生…生, 身…身, 私…私."
  }
